written("w3_heavier_falls_faster_claim", 3, 5, "3m",
        stem="A student drops a stone and a sheet of paper and sees the stone land first. She concludes: 'Heavier objects always fall faster.' Evaluate her conclusion.",
        points=[P("the conclusion is wrong: in a vacuum all objects fall with the same acceleration g, because a = mg ÷ m", ["wrong", "incorrect", "not correct", "disagree", "same acceleration", "vacuum", "a = g", "mass cancels", "fall together"]),
                P("the paper lands later because air resistance is large compared with its small weight, so its resultant force and acceleration are small", ["air resistance", "large compared with its weight", "small weight", "resultant force is small", "slowed by the air", "reaches terminal velocity quickly"]),
                P("a fair test would remove air resistance (vacuum tube) or compare objects of the same shape, e.g. scrunch the paper into a ball", ["vacuum tube", "scrunch", "same shape", "ball", "remove air", "without air", "fair test", "same size and shape"])],
        min_points=2,
        model_answer="Her conclusion is wrong. In a vacuum all objects fall with the same acceleration, because a = F ÷ m = mg ÷ m = g regardless of mass. The paper lands later only because air resistance on it is large compared with its small weight, so the resultant force and acceleration are small. A fair test would remove air resistance, for example in a vacuum tube, or scrunch the paper into a ball so that both objects have a similar shape.",
        hints=["What would happen if the same experiment were done in a vacuum?",
               "Why is the paper affected so much more by the air than the stone?",
               "Say whether the conclusion holds, explain the paper's slow fall, and suggest a fairer test."],
        keywords="wrong; same acceleration in vacuum; air resistance large relative to paper's weight; fair test",
        sample_pass=["Wrong: in a vacuum both would fall together with acceleration g; the paper falls slowly because air resistance is large compared with its weight, so scrunch it up or use a vacuum tube",
                     "her conclusion is incorrect; air resistance slows the paper much more than the stone, and without air all objects accelerate at g"],
        sample_fail=["Correct, gravity pulls harder on heavy things so they fall faster", "The stone is more aerodynamic so it is heavier"])

written("w3_tyres_friction_tradeoff", 3, 5, "3l",
        stem="Car tyres are designed to have high friction with the road, yet engineers also try to reduce friction inside the engine. Explain why both aims make sense.",
        points=[P("friction between tyres and road is useful: it provides the grip for the driving force, for braking and for cornering without skidding", ["grip", "driving force", "braking", "brake", "cornering", "steer", "skid", "push against the road", "accelerate", "stop"]),
                P("friction inside the engine is a nuisance: it opposes the motion of the parts, wastes energy as heating and causes wear", ["wastes energy", "heat", "heating", "wear", "opposes the motion", "slows the parts", "less efficient", "reduces efficiency"]),
                P("so lubricating oil is used to reduce engine friction while tyre tread and rubber keep road friction high", ["oil", "lubricat", "tread", "rubber", "grooves", "reduce engine friction", "keep road friction high"])],
        min_points=2,
        model_answer="Friction between the tyres and the road is useful: it provides the grip that transmits the driving force, lets the brakes slow the car and allows it to corner without skidding. Friction between moving parts inside the engine is a nuisance: it opposes their motion, wastes energy by heating them and wears them out. Engineers therefore use lubricating oil to reduce engine friction, while rubber and tread patterns keep road friction high.",
        hints=["Friction opposes relative motion. Where is that helpful in a car, and where is it harmful?",
               "Think about grip and braking versus heat and wear.",
               "Explain the useful friction, the wasteful friction, and how each is managed."],
        keywords="tyre friction gives grip for driving/braking/cornering; engine friction wastes energy and wears parts; oil",
        sample_pass=["Tyre friction gives grip for accelerating, braking and cornering, but friction in the engine wastes energy as heat and wears parts, so oil is used to reduce it",
                     "road friction is needed to stop and steer without skidding; engine friction opposes the moving parts and heats them, so it is reduced by lubrication"],
        sample_fail=["Friction is always a bad thing", "Tyres need friction so the car is heavy"])

